<h1>2. Housing and Equipment</h1>
<p class="lead">Broilers have no tolerance for bad housing. Get the basics right and the batch runs itself.</p>
<div class="meta"><strong>Module 2 of 6</strong> · Broiler Farming for Profit · quiz at the end</div>
<h2>Lesson 2.1 - Floor space</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Age</th><th>Birds/m2</th><th>3m x 4m holds</th></tr>
<tr><td>Week 1-2</td><td>20-25</td><td>240-300</td></tr>
<tr><td>Week 3</td><td>15</td><td>180</td></tr>
<tr><td>Week 4-5</td><td>12</td><td>144</td></tr>
<tr><td>Week 6-7 (finish)</td><td>10</td><td>120</td></tr>
</table>
<p>For 200 birds you need 20 m2 at finish - a 4m x 5m house. Overstocking above 12/m2 reduces final weight by 10-20% and increases mortality.</p>
<h2>Lesson 2.2 - House design</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Open sides.</strong> Wire mesh on 2-3 sides. Airflow is critical.</li>
<li><strong>Roof overhang 60 cm minimum.</strong> Rain and sun protection.</li>
<li><strong>Mesh 2 cm x 2 cm.</strong> Keeps out wild birds and rats.</li>
<li><strong>Concrete or raised floor.</strong> Damp floor = disease.</li>
<li><strong>Roll-down curtains.</strong> For cold nights and the first 2 weeks of brooding.</li>
<li><strong>External feed access.</strong> Do not walk through the flock to feed.</li>
</ol>
<h2>Lesson 2.3 - Litter</h2>
<p>Depth 8-10 cm at start. Top up as needed. Best materials: wood shavings, rice hulls, chopped straw. Do not use wet, mouldy, or chemically treated material. Remove caked litter around drinkers daily.</p>
<h2>Lesson 2.4 - Feeders and drinkers</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Equipment</th><th>Birds/unit</th><th>Cost</th></tr>
<tr><td>Chick feeder (60 cm)</td><td>30 chicks</td><td>3-6</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tube feeder 10 kg</td><td>25-30 birds</td><td>8-15</td></tr>
<tr><td>Chick drinker 1 L</td><td>25 chicks</td><td>2-4</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bell drinker 10 L</td><td>40 birds</td><td>10-18</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nipple drinker line</td><td>8 birds/nipple</td><td>0.50/nipple</td></tr>
</table>
<p>For 200 birds at finish: 8 tube feeders + 5 bell drinkers. Raise feeders and drinkers as birds grow - lip level with bird's back.</p>
<div class="callout"><strong>The ventilation rule:</strong> Ammonia smell at bird height = more airflow needed. Open more of the house. Poor ventilation costs more weight than any feed issue.</div>
<div class="good"><strong>Practical exercise 2.1:</strong> Draw your house. Mark feeders, drinkers, and the prevailing wind direction.</div>
